            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified the following air leakage (heat loss) locations:

** Around the attic hatch. Currently the attic hatch does not seat well, causing the hatch itself to not sit flush on the built frame. It appears the box has shifted inwards, making it so the hole is smaller than the hatch door. Once the box has been fixed (and the hatch door sits correctly), you can seal the hatch door with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack"

** The open combustion air pipe (be the header space of mechanical room) and boiler flue, as well as the old (unused) flue on the back wall in the mechanical room.  An electronic damper could be installed on the combustion air pipe. This will ensure combustion air is only being intriduce into the mechanical when the boiler is operating (and closed while off). If you consider upgrading your heating system with a sealed combustion unit (see heating section of this report), this pipe will no longer be required. 

** The open plumbing in the bathroom which is currently being renovated

** Around several of the window frames. I recommend removing the trim and filling the gap (between the window frame and wall) with low expansion sprayfoam

** Air leakage was also observed through both of the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

Savings: I modeled a 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 130 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.5 GJ, and qualify for $ 500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). Achieving a minimum 20% reduction qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. I recommend you add a minimum R10 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.

Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).

Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 270 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add a R15 or more, both your rebate &amp; energy savings will increase.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic space of your home appears to have a total of R28 of insulation, comprised of blown cellulose insulation. I have modeled the energy savings if you were to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R50. Please be sure you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting.

Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R28 up to a minimum R50, this is estimated to save $ 200 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.4 GJ, and qualify for a $ 250 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to a $125 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

The basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with mostly 2x6 (R20) framed interior walls (the mechanical room appears to be about R8). 



Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 framing with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. In conjuction with the above main wall insulation upgrade, have modelled adding R10 to the pony walls. 



Savings: By adding R10 insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially save $  per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by XX GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 2,500.00 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to a $ 1,250.00 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

The majority (10) of the windows in this house are older double pane vinyl windows, that are in reasonable shape (with the exception of the one bedroom window with a broken pane). I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.

Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 430 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 17.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $2,500 in rebates (10 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for window upgrades.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil boiler. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (adding more insulation and reducing the air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.This upgrade and savings also include the increase in domestic hot water efficiency by continuing to use your boiler to heat your hot water. 

Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $ 850 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 36.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for 50% up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 5 per fiscal year) from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate. 

If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 750 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $10,750 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the wall insulation

     - $2,500 for the windows

     - $250 for the attic insulation

     - $2,500 for the heating system

     - $500 for the air leakage reduction

     - $2,000 for the bonus rebate for reducing overall GJ rating by 30%



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the attic &amp; foundation insulation and the heating system, however these would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see section further down



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.3 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil boiler vent ups a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a 300 CFM range hood   

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Yellowknife

The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



